package p1:module@1.0.0;

/// Running commands. Owner: the process service extracted from `p1-tool-shell`.
///
/// The execution boundary stays native (ADR-0035): the host decides whether a command runs
/// inside bubblewrap, rebuilds its environment from the allow-list (the command never
/// inherits p1's environment), starts it in its own process group in the workspace root with
/// no terminal and no input, and kills the whole group on timeout, cancellation or drop. A
/// module chooses only the command text and its time limit.
interface process {
    record command {
        /// Run as `bash -lc <script>`.
        script: string,
        /// Wall-clock limit in milliseconds; the host kills the process group when it expires.
        timeout-ms: u64,
    }

    /// How a command ended.
    variant exit-status {
        /// The shell exited with this code.
        code(s32),
        /// The shell was ended by this signal.
        signal(s32),
        /// Ended by a signal the host could not name.
        unknown-signal,
        /// The host killed it at `timeout-ms`.
        timed-out,
        /// The host killed it because the call was cancelled.
        cancelled,
    }

    variant process-event {
        /// Output bytes, standard output and standard error merged in arrival order.
        output(list<u8>),
        /// The terminal event: no output follows.
        exited(exit-status),
    }

    /// A running command: a streaming resource.
    ///
    /// `next` blocks the module while the host waits for output. It returns `output` events,
    /// then exactly one `exited`, then `none`; calling `next` again after `none` traps. When
    /// the call is cancelled while `next` is blocked, the host kills the process group and
    /// `next` returns the remaining output, then `exited(cancelled)`. Dropping the resource
    /// kills the process group if it still runs. When the host abandons the call while `next`
    /// is blocked (an epoch deadline, or the caller dropping the call), it drops the resource
    /// itself with the same effect, and it does so too when the call returns with the
    /// resource still held (a later use traps), so no command outlives the call that started
    /// it. A trap never undoes what the command already did.
    resource running {
        next: func() -> option<process-event>;
    }

    /// Start `command`. `err` names why it could not start (the shell or the sandbox is
    /// missing); nothing runs then.
    spawn: func(command: command) -> result<running, string>;
}
